1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,550
Card & Krueger (1994): real data — New Jersey’s minimum wage and fast-food jobs

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:08,950
April 1992: New Jersey raised its minimum wage from $4.25 to $5.05 an hour. Neighbouring eastern Pennsylvania did not.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,950
Card & Krueger surveyed 410 fast-food restaurants before (February–March) and after (November–December). Starting wages rose only in New Jersey.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,950
Full-time-equivalent employment per store: Pennsylvania fell from 23.33 to 21.17, New Jersey rose from 20.44 to 21.03.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,450
DiD: (21.03 − 20.44) − (21.17 − 23.33) = +2.75 workers per store (store-clustered standard error 1.31). No sign of job losses.

6
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:37,950
These numbers are recomputed here from the original survey data. The finding started a long debate (Neumark & Wascher 2000; Card & Krueger 2000).
